1. Add undo and redo so accidental edits can be reversed and later features
   such as replace and cut/paste are recoverable. Start with one edit per undo
   step; redo reapplies an undone edit, and a new edit clears redo history.
   Grouping consecutive typing into one undo step can come later.

2. Add replacement to the existing search. Use its literal, case-sensitive
   matching: prompt for search and replacement text, then offer to replace
   or skip each match. Reject an empty search, but allow an empty replacement to
   delete a match. Make each replacement undoable and refuse changes in -R mode.
   Replace-all, regular expressions, and case-insensitive matching can wait.

3. Add selection and cut, copy, and paste.

4. Handle UTF-8 text and tabs correctly, including cursor and screen columns.
   gg currently treats a byte offset as a screen column. That works for ASCII,
   but a UTF-8 character can use multiple bytes while occupying one or two
   terminal columns, and a tab uses one byte while spanning several columns.
   Track the mapping between file byte positions and displayed columns so
   drawing, cursor movement, and horizontal scrolling stay aligned; never
   place the cursor in the middle of a multibyte character.

5. Warn before saving if the file has changed on disk since gg opened or last
   saved it. Otherwise a save could silently overwrite another program's
   changes. Let the user decide whether to overwrite the newer file.

6. Add Vim-style parenthesis and bracket matching for (), [], and {}. Highlight
   the delimiter at the cursor and its matching partner, and provide a command
   to jump between them. As in Vim, if the cursor is not on a delimiter, the
   jump should use the next one on the current line. Handle nested pairs and
   matches across lines; follow Vim's rules for strings, comments, and unmatched
   delimiters. Use Vim's motion and matchparen documentation as the reference:
   https://vimhelp.org/motion.txt.html and https://vimhelp.org/pi_paren.txt.html.
   Use Ctrl-Shift-% for the jump, keeping plain % available for typing; verify
   what macOS and Linux terminals actually send for that key combination.
